    icon: "6️⃣",
    title: "IPv6 Fundamentals",
    bodyHtml: `<h3 class="holo-text" style="margin-bottom:12px">IPv6 — The Next Generation</h3>
<p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-bottom:16px">IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses (vs 32-bit IPv4), providing 340 undecillion addresses. It's the future of networking.</p>

<h4 style="margin:12px 0 8px;">IPv6 vs IPv4</h4>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;">
<tbody><tr style="background:rgba(99,102,241,0.1);"><th style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Feature</th><th style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">IPv4</th><th style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">IPv6</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Address Size</td><td style="padding:6px;">32 bits</td><td style="padding:6px;">128 bits</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Format</td><td style="padding:6px;">Dotted decimal (192.168.1.1)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Hex colon (2001:db8::1)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Total Addresses</td><td style="padding:6px;">~4.3 billion</td><td style="padding:6px;">~340 undecillion</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Broadcast</td><td style="padding:6px;">Yes</td><td style="padding:6px;">No (uses multicast)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">NAT</td><td style="padding:6px;">Common</td><td style="padding:6px;">Not needed (enough addresses)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Auto-config</td><td style="padding:6px;">DHCP</td><td style="padding:6px;">SLAAC + DHCPv6</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--glass-border);">Header</td><td style="padding:6px;">Variable (20-60 bytes)</td><td style="padding:6px;">Fixed (40 bytes, simpler)</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h4 style="margin:16px 0 8px;">IPv6 Address Types</h4>
<div class="neu-inset" style="padding:12px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.8;">
<strong style="color:#3b82f6;">Global Unicast (GUA):</strong> 2000::/3 — Public, routable (like public IPv4)<br>
<strong style="color:#10b981;">Link-Local:</strong> fe80::/10 — Auto-generated, same subnet only (always starts fe80::)<br>
<strong style="color:#f59e0b;">Unique Local (ULA):</strong> fc00::/7 — Private (like RFC 1918 in IPv4)<br>
<strong style="color:#8b5cf6;">Multicast:</strong> ff00::/8 — One-to-many (replaces broadcast)<br>
<strong style="color:#ef4444;">Loopback:</strong> ::1/128 — Same as 127.0.0.1 in IPv4<br>
<strong style="color:#6b7280;">Unspecified:</strong> ::/128 — Same as 0.0.0.0
</div>

<h4 style="margin:16px 0 8px;">IPv6 Shortening Rules</h4>
<div class="neu-inset" style="padding:14px;font-size:13px;">
<strong>Full:</strong> <code>2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001</code><br>
<strong>Rule 1 — Drop leading zeros:</strong> <code>2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1</code><br>
<strong>Rule 2 — Replace consecutive 0s with ::  (once only):</strong> <code>2001:db8::1</code>
</div>`,
